// What a call answers, through the program's wrappers.
//
// A fact read off a call to a known API — a start's fresh process, a
// monitor's ref, a timer's ref — is lost when the program calls the API
// through a function of its own: `def start_transmitter(opts), do:
// DynamicSupervisor.start_child(sup, spec)`, a default-argument wrapper
// in front of it, `{:ok, pid} = start(); {:ok, pid}`. This is the one
// reading of such a wrapper: a function every way of whose returning a
// value hands back what one of its calls answered (answers_call, from
// Argus.Extractor.Answers: the answer itself, its payload — the pid of
// an `{:ok, pid}` — or that payload re-wrapped), followed through as
// many of the program's functions as it takes to calls outside the
// program: the origins.
//
// It is a must-reading. answers_call is all or nothing per function, so
// a function's origins are every value it can answer; and a function
// that calls, on one of its ways out, a function of the program that
// answers nothing (a lookup of a running process, a literal, a
// parameter), or answers too deep a payload, is opaque: nothing is
// concluded of it. A rule may then conclude from the origins what the
// answer is — a process one of them just started — where points-to's
// may-reading (processes.dl's fresh_return) says what it may be.
//
// A consumer seeds `answer_demand(site, func)` with the calls whose
// answers it asks about, and reads `call_origin(site, origin, depth)`
// for each origin (`depth` counts the payloads taken on the way, 0 or 1:
// a deeper element, such as the pid of `{:error, {:already_started,
// pid}}`, is no answer; add the consumer's own depth and keep the sum at
// most 1) and `call_opaque(site)` for a call whose answers are not all
// known.
//
// Prerequisites: include clientlib/imports.dl (call_instr) before this
// file; answers_call is a Layer-2 input.

// One of the calls outside the program (a library's, a BIF) whose
// answer `func` may hand back, `depth` payloads down, through the
// program's wrappers.
.decl wrapper_answer(func: symbol, origin: symbol, depth: number)

wrapper_answer(g, d, k) :- answers_call(g, d, k), !call_instr(g, _, d).

wrapper_answer(g, o, k + j) :-
  answers_call(g, d, k),
  call_instr(g, h, d),
  wrapper_answer(h, o, j),
  k + j <= 1.

// A function that answers calls, one of which leads to no origin: a call
// to a function of the program that answers nothing, or to one whose
// origin is too deep a payload down.
.decl answer_opaque(func: symbol)

answer_opaque(g) :- answers_call(g, d, _), call_instr(g, h, d), !answers_call(h, _, _).
answer_opaque(g) :- answers_call(g, d, _), call_instr(g, h, d), answer_opaque(h).

answer_opaque(g) :-
  answers_call(g, d, k),
  call_instr(g, h, d),
  wrapper_answer(h, _, j),
  k + j > 1.

// The calls a consumer asks the answers of, with the function they are in.
.decl answer_demand(site: symbol, func: symbol)

// The call at `site` may answer what the call at `origin` answered,
// `depth` payloads down: itself, when it is a call outside the program;
// an origin of the function it calls, when that function answers calls.
.decl call_origin(site: symbol, origin: symbol, depth: number)

call_origin(c, c, 0) :- answer_demand(c, f), !call_instr(f, _, c).
call_origin(c, o, j) :- answer_demand(c, f), call_instr(f, g, c), wrapper_answer(g, o, j).

// A call whose answers are not all known: to a function of the program
// that answers nothing, or an opaque one.
.decl call_opaque(site: symbol)

call_opaque(c) :- answer_demand(c, f), call_instr(f, g, c), !answers_call(g, _, _).
call_opaque(c) :- answer_demand(c, f), call_instr(f, g, c), answer_opaque(g).
